Relevant columns and their potential usage
• form_log."session": groups all page views belonging to the same browser session.  
• form_log."path": lets us know exactly which page was shown (e.g. ‘/regist/input’, ‘/regist/confirm’).  
• form_log."stamp": the time‑stamp we can use to determine visit order (‘input’ first then ‘confirm’ later).  

Typical conditions we will have to use
1. path  = '/regist/input'                         → find the page where the form is filled in.  
2. path  = '/regist/confirm'                       → find the page where it is confirmed.  
3. stamp (input) < stamp (confirm)                 → be sure the input page was visited first.  

Below are ten exploratory → analytical queries that build up to the final answer.  
Each query is limited to 20 rows just to inspect data samples while you work.

```sql
-- 1) Quick look at form‑log rows that contain the word “regist”
SELECT *
FROM   form_log
WHERE  path LIKE '/regist/%'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 2) See the list of different registration‑related paths
SELECT DISTINCT path
FROM   form_log
WHERE  path LIKE '/regist/%'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Sessions that reached “/regist/input”
SELECT DISTINCT session
FROM   form_log
WHERE  path = '/regist/input'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Sessions that reached “/regist/confirm”
SELECT DISTINCT session
FROM   form_log
WHERE  path = '/regist/confirm'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Sessions that reached BOTH pages (order still unknown)
SELECT DISTINCT fi.session
FROM   form_log AS fi
JOIN   form_log AS fc
       ON  fi.session = fc.session
WHERE  fi.path = '/regist/input'
  AND  fc.path = '/regist/confirm'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 6) For each session show the earliest input‑timestamp
--    and earliest confirm‑timestamp (if any)
SELECT   session,
         MIN(CASE WHEN path = '/regist/input'   THEN stamp END) AS first_input_ts,
         MIN(CASE WHEN path = '/regist/confirm' THEN stamp END) AS first_confirm_ts
FROM     form_log
WHERE    path IN ('/regist/input', '/regist/confirm')
GROUP BY session
LIMIT    20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Keep only sessions where input < confirm
SELECT   session
FROM    ( SELECT session,
                 MIN(CASE WHEN path = '/regist/input'   THEN stamp END) AS first_input_ts,
                 MIN(CASE WHEN path = '/regist/confirm' THEN stamp END) AS first_confirm_ts
          FROM   form_log
          WHERE  path IN ('/regist/input', '/regist/confirm')
          GROUP  BY session
        ) AS s
WHERE   first_input_ts IS NOT NULL
  AND   first_confirm_ts IS NOT NULL
  AND   first_input_ts <  first_confirm_ts
LIMIT   20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Same as (7) but also list the two stamps for verification
SELECT   session,
         first_input_ts,
         first_confirm_ts
FROM    ( SELECT session,
                 MIN(CASE WHEN path = '/regist/input'   THEN stamp END) AS first_input_ts,
                 MIN(CASE WHEN path = '/regist/confirm' THEN stamp END) AS first_confirm_ts
          FROM   form_log
          WHERE  path IN ('/regist/input', '/regist/confirm')
          GROUP BY session
        ) AS s
WHERE   first_input_ts < first_confirm_ts
LIMIT   20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Window‑function method to pick the first page in each session
SELECT DISTINCT session
FROM  ( SELECT session,
               path,
               stamp,
               ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY session ORDER BY stamp) AS rn
        FROM   form_log
        WHERE  path IN ('/regist/input', '/regist/confirm')
      ) AS q
WHERE  rn = 1                -- the very first page in that session
  AND  path = '/regist/input'        -- it was the input page
  AND  EXISTS (                       -- and later confirm exists
        SELECT 1
        FROM   form_log AS f2
        WHERE  f2.session = q.session
          AND  f2.path    = '/regist/confirm'
          AND  f2.stamp   > q.stamp
      )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) FINAL ANSWER – number of unique sessions that hit
--     /regist/input first and later /regist/confirm
SELECT COUNT(*) AS cnt_unique_sessions
FROM   (
        SELECT session
        FROM   form_log
        WHERE  path IN ('/regist/input','/regist/confirm')
        GROUP  BY session
        HAVING MIN(CASE WHEN path = '/regist/input'   THEN stamp END) IS NOT NULL
           AND MIN(CASE WHEN path = '/regist/confirm' THEN stamp END) IS NOT NULL
           AND MIN(CASE WHEN path = '/regist/input'   THEN stamp END)
               < MIN(CASE WHEN path = '/regist/confirm' THEN stamp END)
       ) AS t;
```

